## Why This Is Asked

This question probes ability to design an end-to-end test with real-world dependencies (MongoDB, external service) and basic backpressure, plus simple workload modeling. It checks how a candidate isolates bottlenecks across validation, DB writes, and external calls.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency measurement
- Workload modeling and pacing
- Bottleneck identification across components
- Basic backpressure strategies and error-rate monitoring

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example k6 script skeleton to simulate signup load
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';

export default function () {
  http.post('https://api.example.com/signup', JSON.stringify({ email: 'user@example.com', password: 'x' }), {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you differentiate bottlenecks between DB writes and external risk-service calls?
- What metrics would you automate alerting on, and how would you adjust tests if latency targets drift?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of serverless vs. containerized performance under real-world burst scenarios, including cold-start effects, multi-region observability, and cost trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Cold-start impact on tail latency
- End-to-end latency measurement (P99)
- Autoscaling behavior across regions
- Cost implications of burst traffic
- Data strategy for realistic workloads

## Code Example

```javascript
async function measureLatency(url, trials = 100) {
  const start = Date.now();
  for (let i = 0; i < trials; i++) {
    await fetch(url, { cache: 'no-store' });
  }
  const end = Date.now();
  return (end - start) / trials;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you isolate cold-start latency from other backend delays in your telemetry?
- What specific metrics and dashboards would you use to validate cross-region SLA compliance during bursts?

